# Line endings for files that are executed rather than parsed.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows, and two things execute these files on
# Linux: the CI database job, and the container that hosts `psql` when no local
# client is installed. Without an explicit rule, `core.autocrlf` on a Windows
# checkout rewrites them to CRLF in the working tree, and what the Windows
# working tree contains is what a bind mount into a Linux container hands over.
#
# A CRLF line ending in a bash script is tolerated by Git Bash — this was tested,
# not assumed: a CRLF copy of a `set -euo pipefail` script exits 0 under
# `C:\Program Files\Git\bin\bash.exe`. The hazard is not a syntax error, it is
# that a CR can end up *inside* a value: in a heredoc, or in a SQL string
# literal, where it becomes a silent part of the data rather than an error. That
# is the failure mode these rules exist to prevent, so it is stated here rather
# than left to be rediscovered.
#
# Only the executed formats are listed. Applying `text eol=lf` to everything
# would rewrite line endings across the whole tree for no benefit, since JSON,
# YAML and TypeScript are read by parsers that handle either ending.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf

# Migrations are append-only and are applied to a live database, so a change to
# one that has already run is a correctness incident, not a conflict to resolve.
# `-merge` stops git from auto-merging these paths, which surfaces a concurrent
# edit as a conflict a human has to look at, instead of a plausible-looking merge
# that silently rewrites history the platform has already recorded.
supabase/migrations/*.sql -merge
